Stress test for the shorthand-before-longhand sort's correctness, not just one
worked example: `sort_by_shorthand_first` keys every rule by a TOTAL,
per-rule shorthand depth (`Slot_key.depth_of`), not a pairwise "do these two
rules share a property family" comparison - a pairwise comparator that
treats two family-unrelated rules as equal is not a transitive relation, so
a stable sort built on it can leave a real shorthand stranded after its own
longhand whenever enough family-unrelated rules happen to separate them in
the pre-sort (dependency) order.

This fixture pins one arrangement (30 rules: four genuine shorthand/longhand
families - `padding`, `margin`, `border-width`, `inset`; sixteen unrelated
singleton declarations shuffled between them once, with a fixed seed, and
committed here rather than generated at test time) where a pairwise family
comparator gets 5 of the 9 shorthand/longhand pairs below wrong (the
shorthand emitted AFTER its own longhand). Depth orders every one of them
correctly, regardless of how many unrelated rules separate them.

`border-width` is also the property that pins WHY depth must be the
LONGEST path from a root, not the shortest: `border-top-width` is a direct
longhand of both `border-top` (its own, parent-less shorthand - depth 0)
and `border-width` (itself a longhand of `border` - depth 1). The shortest
path (through `border-top`) would put `border-top-width` at depth 1, tying
it with `border-width` and leaving their relative order to chance; the
longest path puts it at depth 2, strictly above BOTH parents, so
`border-width` (1) always sorts before `border-top-width` (2) here.

